            <div xml:id="d89-a2">

                <pb facs="0228" n="132" sameAs="frus:frus1874/pg132"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 2 in No. 79.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Williamson to
                        Señor Soto.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >United States Legation in Central America,</hi><lb/><hi
                                rendition="#italic">Guatemala</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1873-12-21"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >December</hi> 21, 1873</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to reply to your
                    excellency’s note of the first instant, handed me on the eve of my departure for
                    Costa Rica. Inclosed your excellency will find a <pb facs="0229" n="133"
                        xml:id="pg133"/> copy of a memorandum of a conversation on the 10th instant,
                    at Punta Arenas, with President Guardia, an open letter from President Guardia
                    to President Barrios, and a sealed letter from Mr. Edward Corbett; Her Britannic
                    Majesty’s minister, which he read to me before sealing. These papers will inform
                    your excellency’s government of the strongly expressed desire of the president
                    of Costa Rica for peace in Central America, and also of his approval of the
                    proposed personal conference of the five Presidents. I hope all the good results
                    may follow the conference that reasonably may be expected, and redound to the
                    honor of the Presidents of Guatemala and Salvador who inaugurated this seemingly
                    happy plan of settling the differences among the states and their chief
                    magistrates. I also beg to inform your excellency that, on my return voyage from
                    Costa Rica, I sent a copy of the inclosed memorandum to the President of
                    Nicaragua, and handed one to President Gonzalez, whom I had the good fortune to
                    meet at Amapala.</p>
                <p>Renewing to your excellency the assurance of my high consideration, I have the
                    honor to be your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer><signed><list>
                            <item>GEO. WILLIAMSON.</item>
                            <item>Señor <hi rendition="#small-caps">Don Lic’do Marco A.
                                    Soto</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Minister of Foreign
                                    Affairs, Guatemala.</hi></item>
                        </list></signed></closer>
            </div>
